---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import TerminalSnippet from '../../components/TerminalSnippet.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../../utils/paths.js';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Step-by-step setup guide for JobFoundry with Docker, Podman, or standalone Node.js."
  currentPath={resolvePath('/docs/getting-started/')}
  category="Installation & Setup"
>
  <h1>Getting Started with JobFoundry</h1>
  <p>
    JobFoundry runs as a local daemon on your machine or private server. Follow this guide to initialize the server, configure your LLM endpoint, and verify service health.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Runtime:</strong> Linux, macOS, or Windows WSL2.</li>
    <li><strong>Container Engine (Recommended):</strong> Docker or Podman with compose support.</li>
    <li><strong>Manual Node.js:</strong> Node.js version 22 or higher with npm.</li>
    <li><strong>LLM Backend (Optional for scoring):</strong> Local Ollama, vLLM, or any OpenAI-compatible API.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Method 1: One-Line Install Script</h2>
  <p>
    The fastest way to deploy JobFoundry locally on Linux and macOS:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry/main/install.sh | bash"
    title="ONE-LINE INSTALL"
  />
  <p>
    This script verifies container runtimes (Podman or Docker), clones or updates repository assets, initializes <code>.env</code>, and starts the container daemon.
  </p>

  <h2>Method 2: Docker Compose or Podman</h2>
  <p>
    If you prefer cloning the repository and managing services via compose:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="git clone https://github.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry.git && cd JobFoundry"
    title="CLONE REPOSITORY"
  />
  <p>
    Copy the sample configuration and configure your secrets:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="cp .env.example .env"
    title="CONFIGURE ENVIRONMENT"
  />
  <p>
    Start the pipeline services in the background:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="docker compose up -d"
    title="START SERVICES (DOCKER)"
  />
  <p>Or with Podman:</p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="podman compose up -d"
    title="START SERVICES (PODMAN)"
  />

  <h2>Method 3: Native Node.js Setup</h2>
  <p>
    For active development or running without containers:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="npm install && npm test"
    title="INSTALL DEPENDENCIES & RUN TESTS"
  />
  <p>Start the web server and ingestion workers:</p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="npm start"
    title="START PIPELINE DAEMON"
  />

  <h2>Environment Configuration (.env)</h2>
  <p>
    JobFoundry stores settings in a local <code>.env</code> file. The key configuration parameters include:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Variable</th>
        <th>Default</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>PORT</code></td>
        <td><code>8080</code></td>
        <td>Port for the web dashboard and extension ingestion API.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>DATABASE_URL</code></td>
        <td><code>sqlite://./data/jobfoundry.db</code></td>
        <td>Local SQLite database or Postgres connection string.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>LLM_BASE_URL</code></td>
        <td><code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code></td>
        <td>Base URL for your LLM endpoint (Ollama default).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>LLM_MODEL</code></td>
        <td><code>llama3:latest</code></td>
        <td>Model name used for fit scoring and criteria evaluation.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SIMHASH_THRESHOLD</code></td>
        <td><code>3</code></td>
        <td>Hamming distance threshold for detecting duplicate jobs.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Verifying Installation</h2>
  <p>
    Confirm the daemon is listening and healthy by probing the health check endpoint:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="curl -i http://localhost:8080/health"
    title="VERIFY HEALTH"
  />
  <p>
    Once healthy, open <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>http://localhost:8080</code></a> in your browser to view the Kanban dashboard. Next, install the companion browser extension to begin ingesting job postings.
  </p>

  <div class="next-step-box">
    <span class="next-label">NEXT STEP</span>
    <a href={resolvePath('/docs/extension/')} class="next-link">
      Install the Companion Browser Extension →
    </a>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .next-step-box {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-cyan);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .next-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .next-link {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent-cyan);
  }
</style>
